     answer="A fractional CMO gives a Wilmington company senior marketing leadership part-time, without a full-time executive hire. Extra Medium is a marketing agency in Wilmington, and its fractional CMO practice puts a named senior operator in your leadership meetings, running the plan and the spend, with a full studio behind the seat.",
     sections=[["Growth creates the gap", "<p>Wilmington is adding people, rooftops and competitors at a pace that exposes weak marketing fast. The pattern is familiar: a healthcare practice, a home services company, a hospitality group or a professional firm grows on reputation, while its marketing grows by accretion. A website from one vendor, an ads account from another, a part-timer posting when there is time, and the founder or managing partner still acting as the default CMO. It works until a well-run competitor arrives, or the next neighborhood opens and nobody senior owns the plan for winning it. That gap, between spend and direction, is what a fractional CMO closes. The model is set out on our <a href='/fractional-cmo/'>fractional CMO</a> page.</p>"], ["What the seat runs in Wilmington", "<p>The seat owns the plan across both of Wilmington&rsquo;s calendars: the year-round local market and the visitor season that swells it. That means one strategy the whole company argues from, vendors and freelancers briefed and judged against it, a brand that keeps pace with what the company has actually become, and reporting to leadership against revenue rather than activity. It also means someone senior in the room when the growth decisions get made: a second location, a new service line, a push into the beach towns or up the coast toward Hampstead and Leland.</p>"], ["Shape, cadence and cost", "<p>Engagements run monthly, typically one to three days a week of senior time. For context, published US market ranges for fractional CMO retainers typically run $3,000&ndash;$20,000 a month depending on cadence and scope; that is a market range, not our price. Extra Medium shapes each engagement in the first conversation and puts the shape and the exact price in writing before work begins, with the studio available behind the seat so strategy and execution do not live in different companies. Our <a href='/fractional-cmo-cost/'>cost page</a> breaks down how the pricing works.</p>"]],
     faqs=[["How much does a fractional CMO cost?", "Published US market ranges for fractional CMO retainers typically run $3,000 to $20,000 a month, set by days per week and scope. We do not quote before understanding the company. The first conversation is free, and you receive the engagement shape and price in writing before anything begins."], ["Why not just hire a marketing manager?", "A manager runs tasks; a CMO sets the direction the tasks serve. Most Wilmington companies at this stage need the direction first. Once the plan, calendar and reporting exist, a manager is a great hire, and a fractional CMO can help you recruit and lead them."], ["How do we start?", "In writing. Send a note through our <a href='/contact/'>contact page</a> describing the company and where growth is supposed to come from. The reply is specific: whether the seat fits, what we would do first, and what it would cost."]],
     takeaways=["Fast growth hides a marketing gap: the spend grows, but nobody senior owns the plan.", "A fractional CMO sets the direction first. Hires and vendors work better after that, not before.", "In Wilmington, the plan has to serve two calendars: the year-round city and the summer one."],
